# Silicon: Printing Optimal, Post-von-Neumann Hardware from First Principles

Modern integrated-circuit engineering is the most intricate manufacturing discipline humanity has ever built—and one of the most fragmented. Designing a frontier chip costs $100M–$500M and takes two to four years. Roughly **70% of that cost and schedule** is not spent inventing new architectures or transistors. It is spent reconciling incompatible descriptions of the *same physical object*: an architectural specification in prose, a behavioral simulator in C++, Register-Transfer Level (RTL) code in SystemVerilog, heuristic logic synthesis in a gate optimizer, static timing analysis (STA) governed by hand-written exception files, physical placement and routing, Design Rule Checking (DRC), Layout-vs-Schematic (LVS) verification, and transistor-level SPICE simulation.

Every boundary between those representations is a translation gap where bugs enter, optimality is lost, and verification costs explode.

This page is a **complete, self-contained introduction** to both **Hyperactivity** and **unified post-von-Neumann chip design**—built from the ground up so any reader can follow every idea from first principles, with expandable technical derivations, live `.hypercubical` source, emitted SystemVerilog, and verbatim third-party foundry tool transcripts for engineers who want to inspect every gate, picosecond, and square micron.

